## How third-party code is attributed

A partial import is never a whole-history merge. If we need one file or one commit, we take
that commit (`git cherry-pick -x`, or `git filter-repo --path` on a clone) so the original
`Author:` travels with the code and the message names the original SHA. Moving files changes
the commit hash; that is expected and is why the provenance is written down as well as
carried in git.

Only the people whose code is actually in this tree are attributed. Merging an upstream
branch wholesale would make every author in that project a contributor for code we did not
take, which is not honest. GitHub's contributor graph is a derived view: it counts commits
reachable from the default branch whose author email is linked to an account. We keep the
author email, but cannot force GitHub to link it. For a port or a rewrite, the original
author is credited in the file header and here, and the commit says what it is based on.

